how're the long flight to the other side  of the world in a normal plane is tough  enough so imagine making the journey in  a flimsy microlight it's taken Dave  Sykes for months to travel almost 12,000  miles to Sydney from his home in  Yorkshire unsurprisingly when the opera  house and harbour bridge came into view  it was an emotional moment I could see  the bridge in front of me I thought this  is what I plan for 42 years for this is  the actual final flight of it all and I  just couldn't stop smiling really you  know then flying over the actual bridge  looking at all the people doing the  bridge walk waving at me that was just  something else was that Oh Charlie hard  position it is an incredible achievement  but even more so for Dave he's paralyzed  from the waist down after breaking his  back in a motorbike accident with his  wheelchair strapped to his aircraft  nothing was going to stop this York  Sherman making the trip of a lifetime  not even a horrendous lightning storm  over Burma there was a big flash enough  the aircraft shuddered with them a  lightning strike at the side of me and  blue all the fuses out on the  instruments it got to a point where I  wasn't scared anymore and it was just  trying to survive you know not crash  crossing oceans deserts and jungle Dave  flew over 18 countries and who needs  inflight movies when you have a front  row seat of the best views mother nature  can provide raising money for yorkshire  Zaire ambulance Dave coped with sand  storms and even the threat of being shot  down over Iran now though he's just  ready to head home and in case you're  wondering Dave won't be going home by  propeller power but rather in a normal  airplane as for the microlight well  that's going to be packed up put in a  container and we'll head back to Britain  by see Dave flew all the way to  Australia on just a wing and a chair  something worth considering next time  you complain about being on a long-haul  flight  Jonathan Samuels Sky News S